<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Smolensk</h1><p class="st-meta">1127 CE – 1404 CE (approx.) · capital Smolensk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A wealthy Rus' principality on the upper Dnieper, long fought over by Lithuania and Moscow.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, the old centre of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/krivichs">Krivichs</a>, stood where goods were portaged between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Dvina</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostislav-i-of-kiev">Rostislav Mstislavich</a>, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a>, became its prince in 1127 and made it independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, setting up a bishopric in 1136. His descendants, the Rostislavichi, ruled it for almost three centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> grew rich from trade with the Baltic, and in 1229 its prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-davydovich">Mstislav Davydovich</a> made a treaty with Riga and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gotland">Gotland</a> that set rules for German merchants. Its princes were strong enough to compete for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a>. The town built many stone churches, including the surviving Church of Saints Peter and Paul.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> largely escaped the Mongol sack but became a tributary of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Squeezed between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> in 1395, and after a last revolt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yury-sviatoslavich">Yury Sviatoslavich</a> it was annexed outright in 1404. Moscow captured it in 1514.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-smolensk.jpg" alt="Principality of Smolensk" width="320" height="207"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en, following request by and knowledge from Veverve · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Principality_of_Smolensk_1237.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1127 CE – 1404 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Smolensk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rostislav Mstislavich, Mstislav Davydovich, Yury Sviatoslavich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Grand Principality of Smolensk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1266&amp;focus=principality-of-smolensk" aria-label="Principality of Smolensk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5385 303 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Smolensk lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5705 559L5697 681M5705 559L6352 637M5705 559L5556 562M5705 559L5751 548"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0M6352 637h0M5556 562h0M5751 548h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5705 559h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5705 559h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Smolensk. <a href="/atlas?year=1266&amp;focus=principality-of-smolensk">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
